<%# @spec PARTIAL-CLOSEOUT-022 %>
<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-8 sm:px-6">
  <div class="mb-6 flex items-center justify-between gap-4">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Partial closeout operator guide</h1>
    <%= link_to "Back to Inbox", inbox_path(kind: :partial_closeout),
          data: { turbo_frame: "_top" },
          class: "inline-flex items-center rounded-md bg-white px-3 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 ring-1 ring-inset ring-gray-300 hover:bg-gray-50" %>
  </div>

  <p class="mb-6 text-sm text-gray-600">
    A partial closeout Inbox item means Paid recorded terminal evidence for an open issue (a merged partial
    pull request or a no-code-required declaration) and the duplicate-work guards now keep it out of automatic
    scheduling. This guide explains what each recovery action actually does so you can pick the right one.
  </p>

  <section class="mb-8 space-y-3 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Deciding what to do</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm text-gray-700">
      <li><strong>Review the recorded criteria and evidence.</strong> Re-read the issue's acceptance criteria and
        the merged pull requests and originating runs listed in the pane before acting.</li>
      <li><strong>Continue agent-actionable work</strong> when the remainder is code an agent can write. Request a
        deliberate continuation and describe the remaining work and the evidence you expect.</li>
      <li><strong>Create or link prerequisite work</strong> when part of the scope belongs in its own issue. Link
        the prerequisite from the pane, or have an agent file it with the <code>create_issue</code> tool using
        dependency wording.</li>
      <li><strong>Supply human evidence</strong> when the gap needs hardware, credentials, pilot results, or human
        adjudication an agent cannot produce. Follow the requested steps, then link the result.</li>
      <li><strong>Attest completion when justified.</strong> Resolve the item as complete only when the recorded
        evidence genuinely covers every acceptance criterion, and write a rationale that references that
        evidence.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8 space-y-3 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Three different "completions"</h2>
    <dl class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-700">
      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-gray-900">What completes a run</dt>
        <dd>the run's container reaching a terminal status and Paid recording the outcome — a merged pull request or a
          no-code-required declaration stamped on the issue. A completed run is evidence about one execution; it
          says nothing about the issue's whole scope.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-gray-900">What resolves an Inbox item internally</dt>
        <dd>your attestation that the recorded evidence completes the issue. Paid records your identity, your rationale,
          and the evidence generation you resolved against, marks the issue <code>completed</code> internally, and
          suppresses this Inbox item. The GitHub issue stays open for a human to close, and new terminal evidence can resurface the item here
          (a newly merged pull request or a new no-code declaration starts a new generation). Resolving also supersedes any open continuation authorization and cancels its queued run, so
          nothing dispatches after your attestation.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="font-medium text-gray-900">A final pull request closes the GitHub issue or epic</dt>
        <dd>only when a human merges it (or a human closes the issue directly). Paid never closes
          the upstream issue for you; an epic additionally stays open until every child scope is genuinely
          accepted.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8 space-y-3 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Requesting a continuation</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
      A continuation request authorizes exactly one run, scoped to the evidence generation current when you
      submit it. Other holds still apply: dependencies, creator trust, feature release and design-revision
      gates, budgets, project and issue pauses, skip labels, needs-input, and manual review each still refuse the
      request with their specific reason. When the run finishes, the guards re-arm — continuing again requires a
      new request against the new evidence.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
      Describe the remaining work and the expected evidence in the reason; the run receives it as its
      remaining-work plan. For example:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-gray-700">
      <li><strong>fresh acceptance audit:</strong> "Re-check the acceptance criteria against merged PR #31 and
        report which criteria remain unmet, with evidence for each."</li>
      <li><strong>implementation gap:</strong> "PR #31 shipped the API but not the CLI flags; implement the flags
        and link the passing command output."</li>
      <li><strong>scanner verification:</strong> "The alert recurrence needs new remediation; fix the finding and
        wait for the default-branch scan to confirm."</li>
      <li><strong>human-only evaluation:</strong> only when a human decision genuinely gates the work — the run
        should stop and report rather than wait for input that is not coming.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8 space-y-3 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Human prerequisites and evidence</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
      A human prerequisite names the requested evidence, who must supply it, and the exact next step. Supply the
      evidence the step describes (usually by attaching or commenting the measured result, credential grant, or
      adjudication on the prerequisite), then link that follow-up work to this issue so the dependency is
      recorded.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
      What resumes automatically: when an agent-owned prerequisite closes, ordinary scheduling picks the parent
      back up. What requires a deliberate continuation: Paid never retries an agent for unavailable human evidence — after the evidence exists, request the continuation yourself and say what the
      run should do with it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8 space-y-3 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">Linking prerequisites</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
      The pane's link action appends <code>Depends on #N</code> wording to the stalled issue on GitHub and
      refreshes Paid's dependency records immediately, showing the successful linkage. Paid must already know the
      prerequisite issue — if it was just created on GitHub, sync the project first. A sync is likewise required
      to pick up dependency wording edited directly in a body on GitHub.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8 space-y-3 rounded-xl border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50 p-5">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-amber-900">Audits, gap transfer, and epic acceptance are different things</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-amber-900">
      <li>An audit closes only when its measured results are recorded against its criteria.</li>
      <li>Transferring a gap to a follow-up owner moves the work. A follow-up owner is not completed acceptance evidence — the parent stays open and blocked until the follow-up itself closes with evidence.</li>
      <li>An epic is accepted only when every child scope carries its own evidence. Closed child issues alone are
        not sufficient evidence.</li>
      <li>If the scope itself was wrong, an explicitly authorized scope revision can retire a requirement — but it
        preserves ownership of the outstanding requirement and must be recorded by someone authorized to change
        it. Filing a follow-up is not a scope revision.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>
